{
  "raw": {
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=914591": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - Occupational Therapy",
      "description": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - Occupational Therapy Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 12/10/2026 Full time, permanent | Curtin Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference Number: 1690420 About the Role: Curtin University School of Allied Health invites applications for a dynamic and motivated individual to join our team as Lecturer (Teaching Academic) or Senior Lecturer (offered as either Teaching Academic or Teaching and Research Academic) in Occupational Therapy . This is a continuing fulltime position, tailored for an individual who excels in their field. The role involves coordinating units, developing materials, and designing curricula, teaching and supervising students at various levels, and conducting educational and/or clinical research. The ideal candidate should have experience in clinical practice, teaching/clinical education, and building relationships with industry and the community. Desired capabilities include significant experience in a university setting, particularly in equity programs and supporting diverse students from various backgrounds and age groups, and significant experience in research. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Education: A doctoral qualification in the relevant discipline (or equivalence and professional standing for a Teaching Academic Lecturer role – please see below). Teaching Skills : Demonstrated high level teaching skill and a reflective approach to teaching delivery (including teaching occupational therapy students, colleagues, clients or family members). Pedagogical Knowledge: An understanding of contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline and a record of contribution to scholarship in teaching. Unit Coordination: Demonstrated experience or capacity to undertake the unit coordination role. Communication: Demonstrated high level communication and interpersonal skills with a commitment to the development of a collegial and supportive working environment and the ability to interact with students and staff with cross cultural sensitivity. Policy Commitment: Demonstrated commitment to applying relevant and applicable policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the functions of this position. For those applying for a Lecturer (ALB) position: If you do not hold a PhD qualification, please outline your equivalent experience in your cover letter with reference to the following document: https://tinyurl.com/3r6yfaz4. Please note that if your qualifications or experience look different from what we've described above, but you feel your skills and potential are a match to the capabilities we need, please apply. We are looking for diversity in experience. If you would like to be considered for an ALC level (Senior Lecturer), please ensure that you also add the following criteria in addition to the above: Research Record : An established record of research outputs in high quality refereed academic journals/outlets consistent with the discipline. Grant Applications: A history of competitive grant applications. Supervision: Demonstrated ability to supervise higher degree research students. Leadership: Demonstrated leadership skills with the ability to lead major curriculum and/or research initiatives, and provide mentoring to less experienced staff. Reputation: Evidence of an established national reputation and growing international profile, and a record of obtaining research income, including nationally competitive research grants and/or fellowships. *Please note that each candidate will be assessed based on their skills, qualifications, and experience to determine the salary level. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new team: School of Allied Health The School of Allied Health at Curtin University is a vibrant, collaborative, and community-focused school dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of people across Western Australia and beyond. Our teaching, research, and partnerships span a wide range of allied health disciplines, including exercise and sport science, occupational therapy, physiotherapy, speech pathology and social work. We are deeply committed to delivering an exceptional student experience - one that prepares graduates to be compassionate, skilled, and practice-ready professionals who make meaningful contributions to the communities they serve. Our staff are passionate educators, researchers, and practitioners who work closely with industry partners, health services, and community organisations to ensure our programs remain relevant, innovative, and responsive to emerging health needs. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a full time, permanent position, with a salary range between $121,857 - $144,352 (ALB) / $148,847 – $171,344 (ALC) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) – maximum of four pages o Please indicate in your cover letter which role you are applying for – ALB or ALC. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Sunday, 01 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=914482": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Teaching Focused",
      "description": "Lecturer - Teaching Focused Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Permanent Part Time 12/10/2026 Part-time ongoing role 3 days per week (flexible on days) Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Generous leave entitlements Reference: 1676607 About the Role [GH1] The School of Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry (MCASI) is seeking to strengthen its expertise in the fields of Libraries, Archives, Records and Information Science through the appointment of a Lecturer (ALB level) in a teaching-focused role. We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise or professional experience in organising and describing information resources, applying metadata standards, managing and preserving digital collections, and supporting effective records management practices. In this position, you will contribute primarily to teaching and learning, delivering high-quality postgraduate education and supporting an outstanding student experience. You will work collaboratively with colleagues to design and enhance curriculum, coordinate units, supervise students, and contribute to the ongoing development of the discipline. You will also engage with industry, professional bodies and the broader community to ensure teaching remains contemporary, relevant and impactful. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching, innovation in curriculum design, student success, and scholarship that informs and enhances learning and teaching practice. Your responsibilities will include: Design and deliver high-quality online teaching to postgraduate students. Develop innovative curriculum, learning activities and teaching resources that support student engagement and achievement. Apply research-informed and evidence-based pedagogical approaches to enhance student learning outcomes. Assess student learning and provide timely, constructive feedback. Coordinate units and contribute to course development, review and continuous improvement activities. Supervise honours, postgraduate coursework and higher degree research students, as appropriate. Identify and respond to the diverse learning needs of students, including supporting equity groups and facilitating successful transitions into university learning. Maintain currency in discipline knowledge, professional practice and contemporary higher education teaching approaches. Contribute to scholarship in teaching and learning and engage in professional development activities, including peer review of teaching. Collaborate with colleagues, industry partners and professional organisations to enhance the relevance and quality of teaching programs. Undertake administrative and academic service responsibilities, including moderation, assessment and participation in School and University committees. Contribute positively to a collegial, inclusive and supportive academic environment. Essential Criteria A doctoral qualification in Library and Information Science, Information Studies, Archives and Records Management, Records and Information Management, or a closely related discipline, with demonstrated expertise relevant to libraries, archives, records or information science. Demonstrated high-level teaching skills, including the ability to reflect on and improve teaching practice. A strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary higher education practices relevant to the discipline. An emerging record of contribution to scholarship in learning and teaching. Demonstrated experience in unit coordination, or capacity to undertake unit coordination. Ability to develop engaging curriculum, learning activities and teaching resources that support student learning. Experience supervising honours, postgraduate coursework or higher degree research students, or demonstrated capacity to do so. High-level communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, with the ability to foster a collegial and supportive working environment and engage effectively with students and staff from diverse cultural backgrounds. Commitment to ongoing professional development and maintaining currency in discipline knowledge and teaching practice. Commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the position. Desirable Criteria Demonstrated expertise or professional experience in metadata and information organisation, digital preservation, and/or records management. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Part-time hours worked across three days per week, to be discussed at interview. Teaching activities may be required during summer teaching periods, depending on School needs. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $121,857 - $144,352 Pro-Rata + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) – 2 – 3 pages max. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Thursday 29th of October 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=913050": {
      "title": "Casual Academic",
      "description": "Casual Academic Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Casual 7/10/2026 Associate lecturer ALA UniReady Sessional Teaching Pool Based on our vibrant Curtin Bentley Campus Reference: 1693005 About the Role: Join Curtin University's UniReady sessional teaching pool and help transform lives through education. As a Sessional Teaching Academic, you will support students from diverse backgrounds, including equity cohorts and those who may be underrepresented in higher education, to develop the skills, confidence, and academic capability needed for university success. Through engaging teaching, constructive feedback, and a commitment to inclusive practice, you will make a meaningful difference in students' educational journeys while working collaboratively within a supportive and student-focused teaching team. Your responsibilities will include: Deliver engaging, high-quality teaching and learning experiences that support student success in enabling education in any of the following units (Academic Writing, Communications, Numeracy Essentials, Math for Science, Humanities, Commerce, Health Sciences, Science and Technology) Foster inclusive and supportive learning environments that recognise the diverse needs of students and assist their transition to university study. Assess student learning, provide constructive feedback, and support students in achieving intended learning outcomes. Develop and contribute to learning resources, curriculum delivery, and collaborative teaching practices within the UniReady team. Maintain excellence in teaching through innovation, scholarship, professional development, and ongoing engagement with contemporary enabling education practices. Who We Are Looking For/Selection Criteria: Relevant Qualifications and Disciplinary Expertise: A tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline, with demonstrated knowledge and experience appropriate to teaching in an enabling, higher education, adult learning, or senior secondary education context. Student - Centred Teaching and Learning: Demonstrated experience designing, facilitating, or delivering engaging learning experiences that support diverse learners, including students from equity cohorts and educationally underrepresented backgrounds, promote participation, and contribute to positive student outcomes in enabling education. Commitment to Inclusive and Culturally Responsive Practice: Demonstrated ability to create a supportive, inclusive, and culturally responsive learning environment that values diversity and fosters student confidence, belonging, and success. Supporting Academic Development and Student Success: Experience supporting enabling student learners to develop academic skills, self-regulation, confidence, persistence, and independent learning strategies, particularly students transitioning into tertiary education, including the provision of constructive feedback that promotes growth and achievement. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills, with demonstrated digital literacy and the ability to effectively use learning technologies, work collaboratively within a teaching team, contribute positively to a student-focused culture, and demonstrate a commitment to continuous professional learning and improvement. Remuneration: Remuneration will be in accordance with the Curtin University Casual Academic pay rates, with applicable rates currently ranging from approximately $58.12 per hour (marking) to $174.36 per tutorial delivered, depending on duties performed. Role Requirements: You will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check Your New Team: Centre of Enabling Pathways/UniReady The UniReady team within Curtin University's Centre for Enabling Pathways is committed to helping students from diverse educational and life experiences realise their potential and succeed in higher education. Through supportive, student-centred teaching, our educators foster confidence, academic capability, resilience, and a sense of belonging. We are passionate about creating engaging learning experiences that empower enabling students to transition successfully to university study and achieve their future academic goals. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity please contact Naomi n.jeong@curtin.edu.au on or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Friday 6th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
