    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=913693": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer - Paramedicine",
      "description": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer - Paramedicine Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 8/10/2026 Full time and part time opportunities available on a permanent basis Generous leave entitlements and a variety of salary packaging options available On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Reference Number: 1676031 About the Role: Curtin University's School of Nursing is inviting applications from motivated and dynamic individuals to join our team as an Associate Lecturer / Lecturer (Teaching Academic, for the delivery of the Bachelor of Science (Paramedicine course). The teaching responsibilities include utilising various instructional methods - such as online, distributed, and face-to-face activities - to enhance student learning. Educators are expected to collaborate on curriculum design, assess student needs, provide tailored support, and supervise postgraduate and honours students. Ongoing professional development and effective management of related administrative tasks are encouraged. Additionally, supporting the transition of first-year students to university and ensuring equitable assistance for diverse populations are key priorities. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Registered as a Paramedic with AHPRA registration. Progress towards a doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline (or equivalent accreditation and professional standing for a Teaching Focused Clinical Professional role). For appointment at ALB, a doctoral qualification is required. Demonstrated ability to teach effectively and a commitment to reflective improvement in teaching delivery. For appointment at ALB, high-level teaching skills and a strong reflective approach to teaching delivery are required. An understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline. For appointment at ALB, a strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice is required. Demonstrated willingness and ability to contribute to scholarship in teaching through participation in teaching and learning strategies and professional development in higher education teaching. For appointment at ALB, an emerging record of contribution to scholarship in teaching is required. Demonstrated ability to contribute to curriculum review and renewal. For appointment at ALB, demonstrated experience or capacity to undertake unit coordination is required. Demonstrated high-level communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, with a commitment to a collegial and supportive working environment and the ability to interact with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to applying relevant and applicable policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the position. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction School of Nursing The Curtin School of Nursing is the longest-established nursing school in the Australian university sector. Since opening in 1974, the school has played a central role in Curtin’s leadership in health sciences education and research. Today, we rank in the top 100 in the world for Nursing in the QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026. The school delivers undergraduate courses in nursing, oral health therapy and paramedicine; and specialist postgraduate courses in areas ranging from diabetes and critical care to child health and midwifery. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: Full time and part time opportunities available on a permanent basis , with a salary range between $85,872 - $115,862 (ALA - Associate Lecturer) / $121,857 - $144,352 (ALB - Lecturer) per annum, plus 17% superannuation . Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages Please confirm your preferred work type, full time or part time (if part time, please also state your availability) Please also confirm which level role you are applying for (ALA/ALB). If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Thursday, 05 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=911079": {
      "title": "Psychologists - Multiple Opportunities",
      "description": "Psychologists - Multiple Opportunities Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 1/10/2026 Continuing and Fixed-Term Opportunities | Full-time / Part-time | Bentley Campus Generous leave entitlements plus 17% superannuation Join a large, supportive team of psychologists working collaboratively Job Reference: 1688963 Curtin University is seeking experienced and compassionate Counsellors to join our Psychological and Counselling Services team and support the wellbeing, engagement and success of our diverse student community. We currently have the following opportunities available: 2 Fixed-term positions (0.6FTE and 1.0FTE), commencing ideally this year for approximately 6 months 1 Continuing (1.0FTE) position, commencing this year/early 2027. We recognise that flexibility matters and applications from candidates seeking part-time or job-share arrangements are also welcomed. Suitable arrangements will be considered based on operational requirements and the combination of successful applicants. We also anticipate that further Counsellor opportunities may become available in the near future. As part of this recruitment process, suitable candidates who are not appointed to one of the current vacancies may be placed in a recruitment talent pool and considered for similar future opportunities. About the Role As a Counsellor within Curtin’s Psychological and Counselling Services team, you will provide high-quality psychological assessment, counselling and therapeutic support to students across a broad range of presenting concerns. You will join a large and highly supportive team of psychologists who work closely together, providing an opportunity for collaboration, shared expertise and collegial support that may not always be available in smaller services or agencies. Working as part of a collaborative multidisciplinary environment, you will provide responsive, evidence-based and student-centred support, helping students to manage psychological, emotional and psychosocial challenges and remain engaged with their studies. Key Responsibilities Provide psychological assessment and evaluate the psychological, emotional and behavioural needs of students. Develop appropriate treatment interventions and care plans using evidence-based approaches. Deliver short-term individual counselling and therapeutic interventions. Undertake intake, biopsychosocial and risk assessments, triage, safety planning and case management. Provide face-to-face and telehealth services. Deliver individual, group and, where appropriate, couples therapy. Liaise and consult with University staff, health professionals and external services. Facilitate referrals to external agencies and services where appropriate. Develop and deliver preventative psychoeducational workshops, webinars and programs for students and staff. Contribute to service planning, continuous improvement and the ongoing development of psychological and counselling services at Curtin. SELECTION CRITERIA To be successful in this role, you will have: A relevant postgraduate qualification and eligibility for registration as a Clinical or Counselling Psychologist in Australia, consistent with Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency requirements. Demonstrated knowledge and experience in short-term intervention approaches for clients with high support needs. Strong assessment, counselling and therapeutic intervention skills. Demonstrated ability to support clients from culturally diverse backgrounds. Proven ability to work collaboratively within a multidisciplinary team and liaise effectively with a range of professionals. Well-developed written and verbal communication skills. A strong commitment to inclusive, evidence-based and client-focused practice. Experience in tertiary education, telehealth services and the development or delivery of group programs would be highly regarded. Role Requirements You will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. This role requires that the successful candidate undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check. This role also requires General Registration with AHPRA without conditions. Counselling, AccessAbility and Wellbeing Services at Curtin We support our students at a range of levels, from answering their questions about navigating the campus and settling into university life, to providing them with counselling, resources and advice during crises and other stressful situations. Our Counselling team offers the opportunity to work alongside a strong network of experienced psychologists in a supportive and collaborative environment, with colleagues able to share knowledge, perspectives and expertise across a diverse range of student presentations. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You These positions attract a salary package of $122,699 - $130,758 (G08) per annum, pro rata for part-time arrangements, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW , ensuring you attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s essential selection criteria noted above. As part of your application, you will also be asked to indicate: whether you would like to be considered for the continuing opportunity, fixed-term opportunity, or both; your preferred FTE/working arrangement; whether you would be interested in a job-share arrangement; and whether you would like to be considered for future opportunities through the recruitment talent pool. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday, 25 October 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. In addition to filling the current vacancies, this recruitment process may be used to establish a talent pool of suitable candidates for future Counsellor opportunities at Curtin University. Candidates assessed as suitable through this recruitment process may be considered for similar continuing or fixed-term, full-time or part-time opportunities that become available during the life of the talent pool. Placement in the talent pool does not guarantee an offer of employment. Suitable candidates may be contacted as relevant opportunities arise. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=913127": {
      "title": "Senior Test Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Test Analyst Office of Corporate Services Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 7/10/2026 Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ongoing role, Full-time, Bentley Campus Work-life balance options, inc. potential flexible working from home arrangeme Reference: 1673062 About the Role: Are you an experienced testing professional who enjoys solving problems, partnering with stakeholders and helping deliver digital solutions that work beautifully for users? Curtin is looking for a skilled Senior Test Analyst to bring strong technical expertise, curiosity and a quality-first mindset to a varied portfolio of digital projects. Working across Digital Platforms, you will plan testing across APIs and integrations, while also developing and executing automated tests using Katalon Studio. You will work closely with delivery teams and stakeholders to ensure solutions are thoroughly tested and ready for use. Your responsibilities will include: Plan, coordinate and execute testing and acceptance activities across Digital Platforms. Develop test approaches, test plans, test scenarios and test cases based on requirements, solution design and project risks. Perform functional, integration, API, regression and end-to-end testing across digital platforms and connected systems. Develop, maintain and execute automated tests using Katalon Studio, with a focus on improving regression coverage and testing efficiency. Manage defects through reporting, triage, retesting and closure. Ensure test cases, test data, test evidence and automation scripts meet agreed quality and technical standards. Work closely with developers, Product Owners, Business Analysts and business stakeholders throughout the delivery lifecycle. Provide testing guidance and support to other team members and contribute to continuous improvement of testing practices. Who we are looking for (Selection Criteria): Relevant degree with substantial experience, or an equivalent combination of education and training. Strong hands-on experience in manual and end-to-end testing, including functional, integration, API and regression testing. Experience developing test approaches, test plans, test scenarios and test cases and managing defects through the full lifecycle. Hands-on experience developing, maintaining and executing automated tests using Katalon Studio or similar automation tools. Knowledge of AWS or similar cloud platforms, with an understanding of testing applications, APIs and integrations within cloud-based environments. Strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to work confidently with technical teams and business stakeholders. Ability to provide testing guidance and support to other team members; ISTQB certification and higher education experience are desirable. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new Team: Digital & Technology Solutions (DTS) Our Digital & Technology Solutions (DTS) team is at the heart of Curtin’s digital transformation, delivering smart, secure and innovative technology that enhances the way our students and staff learn, connect and work. Leveraging leading platforms such as Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft and ServiceNow, DTS partners across the University to create seamless digital experiences and practical solutions with lasting impact. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous G07 level role attracts a remuneration of $109,277 - $117,331 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Wednesday, 21 October 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=912934": {
      "title": "Fieldwork Coordinator",
      "description": "Fieldwork Coordinator Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 7/10/2026 Full time, fixed term until 30 June 2027 | Curtin Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration, 17% super contributions and generous leave Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference Number: 1690414 About the Role: Curtin University invites applications for a full-time (1.0 FTE) Fieldwork Coordinator to join the Faculty of Health Sciences on a fixed-term basis until 30 June 2027 . We are seeking an experienced and motivated individual who can commence immediately to lead and oversee the Faculty’s fieldwork placement program and drive positive outcomes for students, staff and industry partners. This position collaborates with fieldwork and unit coordinators to maintain and strengthen industry partnerships, serving as a key interface between partners and students to ensure the smooth administration of placements. The Fieldwork Coordinator oversees the daily operations of fieldwork placements, including organising placement team priorities and overseeing ongoing process improvements. The position also regularly reviews administrative processes, implements improvements with staff, and ensures compliance with university policies and procedures. Additionally, the Coordinator develops and maintains strong relationships with industry partners, liaises with students, staff, and clinical partners to resolve placement issues, and acts as the first escalation point for any placement-related queries. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Effective relationship management skills, with the ability to build and maintain productive relationships with a wide range of professional and industry stakeholders. Well-developed organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and respond effectively to the needs of multiple stakeholders. Effective team coordination skills, with the ability to provide day-to-day leadership, direction and support to staff within a small team. Clear and professional written communication skills, with the ability to prepare concise correspondence and documentation for a range of audiences. Sound judgement and practical problem-solving skills, with the ability to assess and resolve day-to-day issues effectively. Effective collaboration and self-management skills, with the ability to contribute positively within a team and work autonomously with limited direction. Desirable Previous experience in, and understanding of, fieldwork placement within the higher education sector. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a full time, fixed term position until 30 June 2027, with a salary range between $99,213 - $105,251 (G06) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Wednesday, 21 October 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=912925": {
      "title": "Research Design Lead",
      "description": "Research Design Lead Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 7/10/2026 Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Fixed-term full-time contract ending 30 June 2028, Bentley Campus Generous leave entitlements Reference: 1679644 About the Role: The Research Design Lead will steward ACSES’s national program of trials, quasi-experimental designs (QEDs) and other rigorous research studies focused on student equity in higher education. This role will translate nationally significant equity questions into a prioritised and adaptive research program, delivered through ACSES-led work and externally commissioned projects via a competitive process. You will provide specialist advice on trial and QED methodology, help ensure commissioned studies are well designed and fit for purpose, and maintain oversight of the design, delivery and assessment of funded work. The role also supports capability building across the sector by helping equity practitioners design and conduct rigorous evaluations in response to their local priorities. Working under the supervision of the Director, Research and Policy Program, you will collaborate with ACSES colleagues, policy and sector partners, university leaders, researchers, trial and QED experts, equity practitioners and commissioned project teams. Your responsibilities will include: Support the Program Director to develop and maintain a prioritised, adaptive national research program focused on higher education student equity. Work with ACSES colleagues, policy partners and sector stakeholders to align research activity with national priorities and sector needs. Monitor emerging evidence, policy priorities and sector developments to ensure the research program remains current and responsive. Provide specialist methodological advice on randomised controlled trials, QEDs and related evaluation approaches to commissioned study teams. Oversee externally commissioned trials and QEDs by monitoring progress, methodological integrity, quality assurance and delivery against funding agreements. Maintain accurate records of research activity, methodologies and findings, including through ACSES’s public-facing trials and evaluation registry. Prepare reports and communicate research progress, findings and insights to ACSES, partners and the broader equity sector. Build sector-wide capability in trial and QED methodology by helping equity practitioners design and conduct rigorous evaluations in response to local priorities. Undertake other duties reasonably consistent with the role. Who we are looking for/Selection Criteria: Demonstrated expertise in trial and/or quasi-experimental design methodology, including experience in applied or real-world settings. Experience providing methodological advice to research teams, including through governance, oversight or quality assurance processes. Experience developing, contributing to or coordinating a strategic research program involving multiple stakeholders and competing priorities. Experience with, or strong understanding of, competitive grant or commissioning processes in a research or evaluation context. Understanding of higher education student equity, education evaluation, public policy or applied social research contexts. Demonstrated ability to build capability in others, including supporting non-specialist practitioners to design and conduct rigorous evaluations. High-level communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build collaborative relationships across policy, university and sector stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with external partners, commissioned project teams and diverse sector stakeholders to support high-quality research or evaluation outcomes. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and oversight relationships. Ability to work independently and contribute effectively within a small, high-performing team. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. While the preferred location for this role is Perth, Western Australia, remote-based arrangements may be considered for the right candidate. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term G09 level role attracts a remuneration of $142,841 - $150,891 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest (one page) A statement addressing the selection criteria – 2 -3 pages max If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Tuesday 20th of October Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=911071": {
      "title": "Psychologist (Registrar)",
      "description": "Psychologist (Registrar) Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 1/10/2026 0.6 FTE | 24-month opportunity | Flexible working days | Bentley Campus Structured registrar program with approved supervision Generous leave entitlements plus 17% superannuation Job Reference: 1690443 Curtin University is seeking a motivated psychologist to join our Psychological and Counselling Services team as a Counsellor (Registrar). This is a 0.6 FTE fixed-term opportunity for 24 months, commencing in November 2026, and provides the opportunity to undertake an Area of Practice Endorsement registrar program in either Clinical or Counselling Psychology while contributing to high-quality psychological support for Curtin students. Working under the supervision of a Psychology Board of Australia approved supervisor, you will develop your professional practice across a broad range of psychological services while participating in continuing professional development. About the Role As a Counsellor (Registrar), you will work as part of Curtin’s Psychological and Counselling Services team at our Bentley campus, providing psychological assessment, individual and group counselling, crisis intervention, consultation and outreach support. You will join a collaborative multidisciplinary environment and work alongside experienced psychologists and other professionals supporting the wellbeing, engagement and success of Curtin’s diverse student community. A key feature of this opportunity is the chance to undertake your registrar program within a large and supportive psychology team, providing access to experienced colleagues, shared learning and ongoing professional development. Key Responsibilities Provide psychological assessment and evaluate the cognitive, emotional, intellectual and behavioural functioning of clients. Determine appropriate psychological treatment interventions and care planning based on psychological assessment. Utilise trauma-informed, neuroaffirming and culturally safe evidence-based interventions. Undertake accurate and thorough intake, biopsychosocial and risk assessments, triage, safety planning and case management. Monitor client progress through regular evaluation. Deliver face-to-face and telehealth treatment interventions. Provide individual and group therapy. Provide consultation and liaise with academics and relevant stakeholders across Curtin University. Facilitate referrals to external agencies where appropriate. Contribute to professional knowledge through participation in staff meetings and collaborative practice. Contribute to the development and delivery of preventative psychoeducational workshops, webinars and programs for students and staff. Participate in research projects where appropriate to inform clinical practice and contribute to the knowledge base relating to counselling and psychological services in higher education. SELECTION CRITERIA To be successful in this role, you will have: A relevant postgraduate qualification from an accredited Master of Psychology program in Clinical or Counselling Psychology. General Registration with AHPRA without conditions, with eligibility to register for an Area of Practice Endorsement Registrar program in Clinical or Counselling Psychology. Demonstrated knowledge of varied short-to-medium term psychological interventions for clients presenting with a range of concerns. Demonstrated ability to conduct intake assessments and apply psychological interventions relevant to the needs of a culturally diverse tertiary student population. Demonstrated ability to work effectively as part of a multidisciplinary team and liaise with a range of professionals. Well-developed written and verbal communication skills, including digital literacy. Demonstrated understanding of the needs of university students. A commitment to evidence-based, inclusive and culturally safe psychological practice. Role Requirements You will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of the role. This is a part-time (0.6FTE) position, with flexibility in working days which can be discussed during assessment. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. You will be required to hold a valid Working with Children Check or have one in the process of renewal. You must hold General Registration with AHPRA without conditions. Counselling, AccessAbility and Wellbeing Services at Curtin Curtin’s counselling services support students experiencing a broad range of psychological, emotional and psychosocial concerns. As part of the team, you will collaborate with colleagues across Psychological and Counselling Services, AccessAbility, Student Wellbeing Advisory Services, Multifaith services and The Living Room, as well as academic and professional staff and external health and mental health services. The Registrar position provides a valuable opportunity to build your professional capability within a well-established service while working alongside a large team of psychologists who can provide collegial support, shared expertise and professional learning. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. What We Offer You This 0.6 FTE fixed-term position attracts a package of $109,277 - $117,331 (G07) with salary applied on a pro rata basis plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a wide range of food options. Curtin also offers generous leave entitlements, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and a range of retail and lifestyle benefits. In addition, this opportunity provides: a structured Area of Practice Endorsement registrar pathway; supervision from a Psychology Board of Australia approved supervisor; continuing professional development; exposure to a diverse range of student presentations; experience delivering both face-to-face and telehealth psychological services; and the opportunity to work within a large and supportive psychology team. Join Us! If this sounds like your next opportunity, please click APPLY NOW, ensuring you attach: an up-to-date Resume; and a Cover Letter outlining your interest in the role and demonstrating how your background meets the essential selection criteria. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday 25 October 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=910556": {
      "title": "Director of Curtin Corrosion Centre",
      "description": "Director of Curtin Corrosion Centre Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 30/9/2026 Competitive Remuneration Package including 17% superannuation Full time, 3-year leadership position | Professorial appointment On-campus Gym, Child Care Centre, Wellness Program, excellent leave and more! Job reference: 1675309 Shape the future of one of Australia’s most established corrosion research centres - advancing research that protects critical infrastructure, strengthens industry and responds to emerging national challenges. Curtin University is seeking an internationally recognised academic leader to guide the Curtin Corrosion Centre through its next chapter of research excellence, industry impact and strategic growth. This is a rare opportunity to lead a Centre with more than 35 years of expertise, longstanding industry partnerships and specialist research infrastructure found nowhere else in Australia. Building on these exceptional foundations, you will set an ambitious future direction - growing the Centre’s research profile, diversifying its funding and expanding its contribution to areas of national and global significance. The Curtin Corrosion Centre Established in 1987 as the Western Australian Corrosion Research Group, the Curtin Corrosion Centre has built an enviable reputation for research that combines scientific excellence with real-world impact. Its work supports the petroleum, mining, defence, chemical processing, maritime, automotive and biomedical sectors, spanning fundamental research, long-term industry collaboration and practical solutions to complex corrosion and materials challenges. The Centre is home to advanced research infrastructure supporting high-pressure and high-temperature studies, environmentally assisted cracking, seawater and atmospheric corrosion, microbially induced corrosion, corrosion under insulation, electrochemistry and corrosion inhibitor testing. Its distinctive capabilities include the EXTREME Laboratory-the only facility of its kind in Australia, enabling testing in sour, hydrogen and mixed-gas environments under extreme pressure and temperature - and Australia’s only laboratory-scale Mono-Ethylene Glycol regeneration and reclamation research facility. The Centre also collaborates with the School of Civil and Mechanical Engineering on dedicated tribocorrosion testing and has access to world-class materials characterisation facilities through the John de Laeter Centre. With an industry-funded Chair and deep, longstanding partnerships across major sectors, the Centre has a powerful platform from which to grow its research in defence, critical minerals, hydrogen, energy transition, renewable energy infrastructure and other emerging areas. Centre staff also contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, industry training and the development of the next generation of corrosion and materials specialists. The Opportunity Reporting to the Head of School, you will provide the strategic, academic and operational leadership needed to position the Centre for continued success and sustainable growth. You will bring people together around a clear and compelling research vision - building on the Centre’s established strengths while opening new pathways for discovery, partnership and impact. A key focus will be growing more sustained, demand-driven research activity through competitive funding, government initiatives, major industry programs and collaborative partnerships. Working closely with Centre staff and leaders across the School, Faculty and wider University, you will connect complementary expertise and identify opportunities to extend the Centre’s capability into adjacent fields of strategic importance. You will nurture the trusted industry relationships that have shaped the Centre’s success while developing new connections across government, academia and the broader research community. As the Centre’s lead ambassador, you will raise its visibility and strengthen its position within the national and international corrosion and materials engineering landscape. In this role, you will: Set and deliver an ambitious research vision aligned with Curtin’s and the Faculty’s strategic priorities. Diversify and grow the Centre’s funding through competitive schemes, government investment, major industry programs and collaborative research. Explore new research opportunities in areas such as defence, critical minerals, hydrogen and renewable energy infrastructure. Build enduring partnerships across industry, government, academia and the wider University. Lead and develop academic, professional and technical staff, creating a safe, inclusive and high-performing culture. Oversee the Centre’s financial and operational performance, including budgets, workforce planning, infrastructure and reporting. Ensure the safe, effective and sustainable operation of the Centre’s specialist laboratories and facilities. Strengthen the Centre’s contribution to undergraduate, postgraduate and industry education. Support postgraduate research training and supervision, helping to develop future leaders in corrosion and materials engineering. Maintain strong research governance and compliance, including requirements relating to work health and safety, foreign interference and export controls. Selection Criteria About you You will be a respected researcher and inspiring academic leader with the vision, credibility and relationships to build on the Centre’s proud history and help shape what comes next. You will bring: A PhD in corrosion engineering, materials science, chemical engineering or a closely related discipline. An internationally recognised record of research excellence and leadership consistent with appointment at Professor level. A strong publication record and demonstrated success in securing competitive and/or industry research funding. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style that empowers others, creates shared commitment and brings about positive change; demonstrable commitment to the University’s values of respect, integrity, courage, excellence and impact. Experience leading and growing a research centre, group or comparable unit, including responsibility for strategy, people, budgets and performance. The ability to build trusted relationships and represent the Centre with credibility across industry, government and academic settings. Experience in higher education teaching and postgraduate research supervision. Experience or established networks in defence, critical minerals, hydrogen, renewable energy infrastructure or another area relevant to the Centre’s future growth will be highly regarded. Experience securing funding through major national research initiatives - including ARC Linkage, Trailblazer or Cooperative Research Centre programs - would also be valuable. Lead the Centre’s next chapter Corrosion research plays a critical role in the safety, reliability and sustainability of the infrastructure and technologies on which communities and industries depend. As Director, you will have the opportunity to lead a Centre with exceptional people, facilities and industry connections - and translate those strengths into an ambitious program of research, education and collaboration with lasting national and international impact. Role Requirements Applicants must hold valid Australian work rights that align with the tenure of the appointment to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace At Curtin University, we are passionate about fostering an inclusive, diverse and welcoming environment in which all people feel respected, valued and safe. Curtin is dedicated to promoting gender equity and inclusivity in all areas. We are proud of our achievements to date and recognise that there is that there is more to do to improve employment outcomes for women and people of diverse gender identities, particularly in STEMM disciplines. As an Athena SWAN accredited institution, we celebrate diversity and actively work to advance the representation of women and gender-diverse people in STEMM areas and empower individuals so they can thrive in their careers through a range of supportive initiatives, including leadership training, mentorship for early career progression and promotion, networking opportunities, and other professional development resources. As reflected in the Athena Swan Charter, gender equality is a broader issue that extends beyond barriers affecting women alone, and we are committed to addressing these challenges holistically. Additionally, we offer staff a competitive remuneration and benefits package, including but not limited to: 17% superannuation, plus a variety of salary packaging options. Paid Parental leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver and 3 weeks for non-primary) subject to eligibility criteria outlined in the Enterprise Agreement; personal leave (14 days) and annual leave (20 days) Enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year (including Easter Tuesday!), and 2 extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year Period (see EBA 51.1 and 51.9). An extra 15 hours (or 2 days) of annual leave each year if you are required to travel to Perth Regional benefits including living allowances and 50% rental subsidy for Curtin‑managed accommodation Breastfeeding facilities Flexible work arrangements including return to work provisions Academic Carer Support Scheme and other diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives detailed via the below link. We believe that diverse perspectives fuel innovation and drive excellence, and we encourage women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people of diverse gender, sex and sexuality, people with disability and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds to join us in shaping the future of STEMM together and making tomorrow better. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. What We Offer You This full-time position attracts a remuneration package of $ 229,829 (ALE) + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ For more information on this positon, please coy and pate the following URL into your browser: https://tinyurl.com/4fza5avw Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your experience aligns with the leadership capabilities, responsibilities and selection criteria noted above. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note, late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Friday 30 October 2026 Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=910558": {
      "title": "Head of School, Population Health",
      "description": "Head of School, Population Health Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 30/9/2026 Competitive Remuneration Package including 17% superannuation Full time, 5-Year leadership appointment based at Curtin's Bentley Campus On-campus Gym, Child Care Centre, Wellness Program, excellent leave and more Job reference: 1677376 Provide strategic leadership to a multidisciplinary School spanning Public Health and Psychology. Shape the School’s future across education, research, partnerships and community impact. Join Curtin’s senior academic leadership community and influence strategy across the University. Curtin University is seeking an outstanding and visionary academic leader to join the Faculty of Health Sciences as Head of School, School of Population Health. This is an opportunity to lead a diverse and highly regarded School whose education and research address some of the most significant challenges facing communities today - from mental health and wellbeing to emerging diseases, climate change, population growth and health inequity. The School of Population Health brings together expertise across Public Health and Psychology to improve health, wellbeing and quality of life locally, nationally and globally. It delivers a broad range of undergraduate and specialist postgraduate programs, alongside short courses that enable health professionals to build new and specialised capabilities. Through its education, research and partnerships, the School prepares graduates to respond to complex and evolving health challenges and make a meaningful difference within their professions and communities. The Opportunity Reporting to the Pro Vice-Chancellor, the Head of School will provide strategic, academic and operational leadership across the School. Working closely with academic and professional colleagues, the Head of School will build on the School’s existing strengths while creating a clear and ambitious vision for its future. We are seeking a highly relational leader who can champion the School’s distinctive identity, disciplines and ambitions while contributing to a connected One Faculty approach. They will look beyond organisational boundaries, bring people together around shared priorities and create opportunities for collaboration across the Faculty and wider University. A key priority will be to further embed equity and diversity across the School’s culture, education, research and decision-making. The Head of School will also advance the School’s contribution to Curtin’s Indigenous strategy, working respectfully with Indigenous colleagues, students, communities and partners to support meaningful and sustained progress. As a senior leader within the Faculty and University, the Head of School will contribute to broader strategy, governance and decision-making - balancing strong advocacy for the School with a genuine commitment to collective success. In this role, you will: Lead and support a multidisciplinary community of academic and professional staff. Shape and deliver the School’s strategic direction and priorities. Advance excellence, innovation and impact across education and research. Strengthen the School’s national and international profile. Build meaningful partnerships with industry, government, academia, the professions and wider community. Develop staff capability and foster an inclusive, collaborative and high-performing culture. Embed equity and diversity throughout the School and strengthen its contribution to Curtin’s Indigenous strategy. Lead financial, workforce and resource planning to support sustainable performance. Guide the School through change and emerging opportunities with clarity, confidence and care. Contribute to shared priorities, leadership and governance across the Faculty and University. About you You will be an accomplished academic and people leader with an internationally recognised reputation in a discipline relevant to the School. You will bring strong academic judgement, a clear sense of purpose and the ability to translate strategy into meaningful outcomes within a large and complex organisation. Highly relational and collaborative, you will understand that the success of the School and the Faculty are closely connected. You will be able to advocate confidently for the School while remaining open to different perspectives, shared opportunities and decisions that support the Faculty as a whole. You will also bring a genuine commitment to equity and diversity, alongside an understanding of the responsibility senior leaders hold in advancing Indigenous engagement, participation and success through respectful relationships, shared accountability and action. We are looking for: A doctoral qualification and an internationally recognised record of academic achievement. A distinguished academic profile with expertise aligned to the disciplines of the School. Significant academic leadership and governance experience within a complex organisation. Demonstrated success in developing and delivering strategic and operational priorities. A thoughtful, inclusive and empowering approach to people leadership. A highly relational leadership style and the ability to build trust, create connections and influence a diverse range of stakeholders. Sound financial, workforce and resource management capability. Experience leading organisational change and building a culture of excellence, collaboration and continuous improvement. Strong industry and community engagement, with the ability to develop partnerships that enhance education, research and impact. A demonstrated commitment to embedding equity and diversity and creating an environment in which people from different backgrounds, identities and experiences can belong and thrive. Current appointment at Associate Professor or Professor level. Leadership at Curtin At Curtin, leadership is grounded in our values of integrity, respect, courage, excellence and impact . The Head of School will be a visible, values-led leader who listens, communicates openly and creates the conditions for others to succeed. They will bring strategic ambition together with a highly relational leadership style - building trust, developing capability and working collaboratively across the School, Faculty and University to deliver meaningful outcomes for students, staff, partners and communities. Role Requirements Applicants must hold valid Australian work rights that align with the tenure of the appointment to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. What We Offer You This full-time position attracts a remuneration package of $ 229,829 (ALE) + 10% Head of School Allowance + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ For more information on this positon, please copy and paste the following URL into your browser: https://tinyurl.com/57xenem7 Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your experience aligns with the leadership capabilities, role responsibilities and selection criteria. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note, late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Friday 30 October 2026 Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=910978": {
      "title": "Project Manager",
      "description": "Project Manager Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 1/10/2026 Fixed term to June 2028, full-time contract, Bentley-based Generous leave + superannuation entitlements On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre & childcare facilities Reference: 1679548 About the Role: Curtin University is seeking an experienced Project Manager within the Australian Centre for Student Equity and Success (ACSES) to lead the Student Outreach and Tracker Project, a national initiative designed to improve the visibility and coordination of university outreach and strengthen evidence-informed planning. The project will bring together contextual and university outreach data to support shared planning and inform the development of an interactive mapping tool. It will also explore the feasibility of linking outreach participation with future higher education outcomes, helping universities better understand and respond to the needs of students and communities, particularly those from underrepresented backgrounds. You’ll take a lead role across project planning and delivery, stakeholder engagement, data collection and standardisation, reporting and evaluation. You’ll also develop data visualisations and dashboards that turn complex information into practical insights to support stakeholder planning and decision-making. Working under the supervision of the ACSES Data Program Director, you’ll collaborate with participating universities, the Project Steering Group, ACSES staff and a broad range of stakeholders to deliver the project and its outcomes. Your responsibilities will include: Lead the planning, coordination and delivery of the project from commencement through to completion, ensuring key milestones and outcomes are achieved. Develop and maintain project plans, timelines, evaluation approaches, budgets, communication strategies and other project documentation. Build productive relationships with participating universities and a diverse range of internal and external stakeholders. Coordinate the collection, standardisation and integration of outreach, school and community data. Support the development and implementation of data sharing arrangements with participating universities. Lead the student tracking feasibility stream, including investigation of legal, privacy, ethical, technical and governance considerations associated with linking outreach participation data to future higher education outcomes. Identify and manage project risks, resources, budgets and procurement requirements. Facilitate stakeholder meetings, focus groups and consultation activities to support project outcomes. Monitor progress, evaluate outcomes and translate findings into clear reports, data visualisations and other project outputs. Provide leadership and guidance to project staff and contractors where required. Who We Are Looking For/Selection Criteria: You’re an experienced project professional who enjoys bringing people, data and ideas together to deliver meaningful outcomes. You’re confident managing complex projects and competing priorities, building relationships across diverse stakeholder groups and turning complex information into clear, practical insights. Extensive project management experience, supported by relevant qualifications or an equivalent combination of experience and education. Demonstrated experience delivering complex projects within defined timelines, budgets and resource constraints. Strong stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively across multidisciplinary teams and diverse stakeholder groups. Experience managing data projects involving data collection, integration, governance, reporting, and visualisation. Strong analytical, organisational and problem-solving skills, with sound judgement and attention to detail. Experience monitoring and evaluating project progress and outcomes. Experience in university outreach, student support, education access or related activities. Desirable Experience working with education, outreach, student equity or related administrative datasets. Experience using and developing maps, dashboards or data visualisations to generate stakeholder-facing insights. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Interstate and/or regional travel may be required. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed term, Full-time G08 level role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume A document addressing the selection criteria for the role If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 9pm (AWST), Sunday, 18 October 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=907023": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - Property",
      "description": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - Property Faculty of Business & Law Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 22/9/2026 Ongoing role, Full-time, Bentley Campus Generous leave entitlements Generous 17% superannuation Reference: 1497749 About the Role: The School of Accounting, Economics and Finance is seeking to appoint a continuing Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in property to contribute to high-quality teaching, curriculum development, research and engagement within the Property discipline. The successful candidate will help students develop the knowledge, professional capability and practical skills required for careers in property, valuation, development, investment, and related fields. Accredited by both the Australian Property Institute and the Royal Institution of Chartered Surveyors, Curtin brings property education excellence and industry experience into the classroom. Strong links with the local property industry connect students with industry and enrich their learning experience. Appointment will be made at Lecturer (ALB) or Senior Lecturer (ALC) level, with responsibilities aligned to the successful applicant’s experience and career stage. Applicants should indicate the level they wish to be considered for. Lecturer applicants should demonstrate capability across teaching, curriculum, research and service, while Senior Lecturer applicants should demonstrate a more established academic profile, including leadership in teaching and/or research, research income, HDR supervision and mentoring. The successful applicant will deliver innovative Property courses, contribute to curriculum development and support an industry- and research-informed learning environment. Their research will complement existing policy-engaged work aligned with housing supply, affordability, sustainability and equity, with opportunities for research funding, multi-disciplinary projects, HDR supervision, leadership and professional development relative to career stage. Core responsibilities across both levels: Deliver high-quality and innovative teaching to undergraduate students demonstrating ongoing reflective improvement in teaching practice. Design and deliver engaging learning materials and assessment activities that enhance student outcomes and foster student learning. Contribute to curriculum design and undertake administrative responsibilities associated with teaching and coordination and contribute to an exceptional student experience. Keep abreast of current property, real estate and related discipline theory, professional practice and contemporary approaches to teaching and learning. Undertake nationally competitive high-quality research in Property and sustain a coherent program of research. Publish in high-quality refereed journals/outlets in manner consistent with disciplinary practice. Co-supervise higher degree by research students. Disseminate research through conferences, seminars, and other scholarly activities including translation of research to students, industry and other external stakeholders. Contribute to research projects and initiatives including competitive research funding as co and/or chief investigator (according to level). Contribute to academic service, collegiality, and effective internal operations. Contribute to external activities relevant to the Property discipline including engaging with industry, the profession, and broader community. Lecturer (ALB) responsibilities: Contribute to the preparation and delivery of courses and units in collaboration with academic colleagues. Contribute to curriculum innovation, content design and the development of learning materials. Coordinate units and undertake associated administrative responsibilities. Support student learning through supervision of honours students, postgraduate coursework students and/or higher degree by research students, as appropriate. Build a research profile through nationally competitive research, scholarly outputs and contribution to funding applications. Contribute to academic service, collegiality and engagement within the School, Faculty, University and relevant professional communities. Senior Lecturer (ALC) responsibilities: Lead or make a significant contribution to high-quality teaching across undergraduate, and honours levels. Lead curriculum development, course coordination and scholarly teaching initiatives within the discipline. Demonstrate an established research profile through high-quality research outputs, competitive funding activity and contribution to discipline standing. Lead research projects or teams where appropriate and actively seek research income as co-investigator or chief investigator. Undertake principal supervision of honours, research masters and doctoral students. Provide academic leadership, mentor less experienced staff and contribute to major School or Faculty initiatives. Foster a collegial and supportive working environment and model a high standard of professional behaviour. Lecturer (ALB) selection criteria: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline. Demonstrated high level teaching skills, including a reflective approach to teaching delivery and student learning. A strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to property, real estate or a related discipline. Demonstrated capacity to contribute to curriculum development, learning material design and unit coordination. A record of research outputs, scholarly activity and/or professional contribution relevant to property, real estate or a related discipline. Demonstrated ability or capacity to supervise honours, postgraduate coursework and/or higher degree by research students. Demonstrated high level communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work collaboratively and interact with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the role. Senior Lecturer (ALC) selection criteria: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline. Proven high level teaching skills, including a demonstrated reflective approach to teaching and student learning. A significant contribution to scholarship in teaching, with experience in curriculum development and unit or course coordination. An established record of research outputs or creative works in high quality refereed academic journals or outlets consistent with the discipline. A clear five-year research strategy designed to deliver research impact and translation. Evidence of a strong national reputation and a growing international profile, including a record of obtaining research income, competitive grants and/or fellowships. Demonstrated ability to disseminate research findings through the media and other sources and to engage industry in the translation of research to impact. Demonstrated leadership skills, including the ability to lead curriculum and/or research initiatives, mentor less experienced staff and supervise higher degree by research students. Demonstrated high level communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to foster a collegial and supportive working environment and interact with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the role. Desirable criteria for both appointment levels: Teaching expertise in at least one of the following areas: property development, property investment and finance, commercial property markets, valuation, or sustainability in the built environment. Research expertise aligned to housing or property, ideally with high-level quantitative skills. Evidence of an emerging or established national research profile, commensurate with level of appointment. Experience engaging with industry stakeholders and delivering quality outcomes. Certified Practising Valuer (CPV) of the Australian Property Institute (API) or Chartered Member (MRICS) of the Royal Institution of Chartered Surveyors (RICS). Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This continuing ALB/ALC level role attracts a remuneration package of $121,857 - $144,352 (ALB) or $148,847 - $171,344 (ALC), plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover letter of no more than 3–4 pages outlining your interest in the role and demonstrating how your background, skills and experience meet the selection criteria for the level you are applying for. Please clearly state in your cover letter whether you wish to be considered for Lecturer (ALB), Senior Lecturer (ALC). If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Monday 19th of October 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=911404": {
      "title": "Program Manager",
      "description": "Program Manager Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 2/10/2026 Fixed term Full-Time Ending 30th June 2028, Bentley-based Generous leave + superannuation entitlements On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre & childcare facilities Job Reference : 1687986 About the Role: Curtin University is seeking an experienced Program Manager to lead the Research and Policy program at the Australian Centre for Student Equity and Success (ACSES). This is an opportunity to manage a nationally significant program encompassing major research initiatives, nationally competitive funding schemes and in-house research focused on strategic priorities in higher education student equity. Working with a diverse range of stakeholders across the higher education sector, you will provide strategic direction, oversee program delivery and ensure initiatives achieve meaningful outcomes. Your responsibilities will include: Lead the planning, management and continuous development of the Research and Policy program. Develop and review strategic program plans aligned with organisational priorities and emerging sector needs. Manage relationships with key stakeholders, negotiating deliverables, managing expectations and supporting successful program outcomes. Monitor and evaluate program performance, identifying opportunities for improvement and ensuring objectives are achieved. Manage program resources and budgets, ensuring effective use of available resources. Provide leadership, guidance and development to staff, including performance management and career planning where required. Prepare high-quality reports, briefing materials and program documentation. Contribute to the ongoing development of a nationally significant program responding to evolving priorities in student equity. Selection Criteria: You are an experienced program or project management professional with the ability to operate strategically while managing complex priorities and stakeholder relationships. Demonstrated experience developing, implementing and managing programs to achieve strategic outcomes within budget and resource constraints. Highly developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders and demonstrate cross-cultural sensitivity. Strong written communication skills, including the ability to prepare clear, concise reports and documentation. Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities while maintaining accuracy and meeting deadlines. Experience working effectively within cross-functional teams, including the ability to provide leadership and guidance to staff. Strong critical analysis and problem-solving skills, with sound judgement and decision-making capability. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This fixed term, full-time G08 level role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) – 2 – 3 Pages Max If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday 25th of October 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=907856": {
      "title": "Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous (s50d)",
      "description": "Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous (s50d) Office of the Vice Chancellor Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 23/9/2026 Inaugural senior leadership role within Curtin's DVC Indigenous portfolio Full-time, five-year leadership appointment | based at Curtin's Bentley Campus Bring people together to support meaningful change Job Reference: 1665640 In line with Curtin’s 2030 Strategic Plan, Curtin University is committed to optimising employment outcomes for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples. Curtin University considers that being an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person is a genuine occupational requirement for this position under section 50(d) of the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA). Therefore, only applications from Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people will be considered. Curtin University is seeking an outstanding academic leader to join our newly established Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous Portfolio as Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous. Working closely with the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous, you will listen to and learn from the people, knowledge and work already present across Curtin, bring colleagues together around a shared vision, and translate the University’s Indigenous strategic priorities into practical academic frameworks, initiatives and outcomes. We are looking for someone with the academic standing and research credibility to lead and influence at the highest levels of the University, but just as importantly, someone who is deeply relational. You will be a thoughtful listener, a trusted partner and a natural connector who understands that meaningful institutional change is built through strong relationships, shared ownership and a genuine commitment to people. While this is a new role, you will be supported by a warm and closely connected team that is genuinely committed to working together and making a difference. The role does not currently have direct reports; however, you will help shape the portfolio’s future capability and, as the work grows, play an important role in building and leading a team. This position will be central to Curtin’s forthcoming Indigenous Strategy and the achievement of one of the University’s key strategic priorities. It is a genuine opportunity to create strong foundations, influence how the University works and contribute to meaningful outcomes for current and future generations of researchers and students. The Opportunity At Curtin, we are deeply committed to placing the voices, knowledges and perspectives of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples at the forefront of our decision-making and creating a University in which everyone can thrive. The Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous will play an important role in bringing this commitment to life across Curtin’s academic activities. Operating under the strategic direction of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous, you will provide delegated University-wide leadership across Indigenous learning and teaching, curriculum transformation, research and Higher Degree by Research pathways, and broader academic capability development. This is both a significant academic leadership position and a highly collaborative role. You will work in partnership with the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic, Deputy Vice-Chancellor Research, Faculty Deans Indigenous, the Centre for Aboriginal Studies, faculties, research institutes and professional portfolios to develop coordinated approaches that deliver meaningful and measurable outcomes. As an inaugural appointment within the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous portfolio, you will have a unique opportunity to help shape the role, establish priorities and contribute to the development of the University's approach to Indigenous academic leadership. Drawing on strengths already present across Curtin, you will identify opportunities, foster collaboration and help create the structures and momentum needed to support lasting change. In this role, you will Provide visible and trusted academic leadership across Indigenous learning, teaching, research and Higher Degree by Research pathways. Help translate Curtin’s Indigenous Strategy into coordinated initiatives that deliver meaningful and measurable outcomes. Champion the embedding of Indigenous knowledges, perspectives and pedagogies across curriculum, research and academic practice. Strengthen Indigenous-led and Indigenous-engaged research, researcher capability, collaboration and impact. Build respectful and reciprocal relationships across Curtin and with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and sector partners. Lead and support a connected, high-performing team while establishing the governance and shared accountability needed to sustain progress. Represent Curtin and advocate for Indigenous scholarship, excellence and success across the University and broader higher education sector. About you We are looking for an accomplished academic leader with the standing, experience and credibility to influence across a complex university environment. You will bring a strong research background and a deep understanding of Indigenous higher education, research and policy in Australia. You will be a builder who is energised by the opportunity to shape an emerging portfolio. You will value and respect the important work already underway across Curtin while being prepared to ask thoughtful questions, connect areas of expertise and develop new approaches that strengthen the University’s collective impact. You will bring: A PhD or equivalent record of scholarly, professional and/or executive achievement leadership, and academic standing consistent with appointment at Professor level. Demonstrated academic leadership and achievement in Indigenous education, research, research training or a related area. Experience leading university-wide academic initiatives and influencing outcomes in a complex higher education environment. A strong understanding of academic governance, including boards, committees and university decision-making processes. A track record of developing and delivering initiatives that strengthen Indigenous participation, success and excellence in higher education. Experience building research capability and/or leading curriculum transformation in an Indigenous higher education context. A strong understanding of the national Indigenous higher education, research and policy landscape. Strong connections with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, communities and cultures. The ability to build respectful, collaborative relationships across faculties, disciplines, communities and stakeholder groups with integrity and collegiality. Experience leading academic and professional staff and developing connected, capable and high-performing teams. Highly developed communication, relationship-building, influencing and advocacy skills. A leadership approach grounded in respect, integrity, cultural safety, care and accountability. A strong commitment to Curtin’s values, strategic priorities and aspirations for Indigenous success. Senior leadership experience within the higher education sector, together with experience working collaboratively with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities to advance education, research and knowledge development, will be highly regarded. If your qualifications or experience look different from what we've described above, but you feel your skills and potential are a match to the capabilities we need, please apply. We are looking for diversity in experience, thought and approach. For more information about this opportunity, including the full position description and selection criteria please review the Candidate Brochure via the following link: https://tinyurl.com/ADVCI-Curtin Role Requirements: You will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. Beyond these benefits, this is an opportunity to: Build something meaningful by creating new programs, partnerships and pathways with university-wide impact. Broaden your leadership experience through engagement with faculties, Academic Board, research leaders and senior executives. Connect education and research across Indigenous curriculum, research excellence, HDR development and academic workforce capability. Join a passionate and well-supported team , working closely with the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous and other senior academic leaders. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit, https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! Please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining: your interest in the role how your background aligns with the leadership capabilities, role requirements and selection criteria If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday 25 October 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=906090": {
      "title": "Associate Professor",
      "description": "Associate Professor Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 19/9/2026 Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Global campuses in Singapore, Malaysia, Dubai, Mauritius & Colombo Diverse & inclusive environment that fosters innovation & collaboration Job Reference Number: 1675320 About the Role: We are seeking an internationally recognised scholar and educator in Space and Planetary Science to join the Space Science and Technology Centre (SSTC) within the School of Earth and Planetary Sciences (EPS) as an Associate Professor. This is an outstanding opportunity for an established leader in planetary science to shape research, teaching and engagement activities within one of the world's leading planetary science groups. The successful candidate will bring an internationally prominent research profile, significant experience working across multiple research institutions and international collaborations, and a demonstrated ability to translate scientific discovery into global impact. The position will play a pivotal role in advancing Curtin's planetary science research agenda through leadership of major research initiatives, participation in international space missions, development of strategic partnerships, attraction of competitive funding, and mentorship of early and mid-career researchers. The successful candidate will also provide academic leadership within the School through the development and delivery of innovative teaching programs in Space and Planetary Sciences, supervision of Higher Degree by Research students, and contribution to the continued growth of Curtin's new Bachelor of Science major in Space and Planetary Sciences. Your responsibilities will include: Provide academic and research leadership within the Space Science and Technology Centre and the broader School of EPS. Lead internationally recognised research programs in planetary science, astrobiology, remote sensing, meteorite science, cometary science, planetary geology or related disciplines. Secure and lead nationally and internationally competitive research grants and collaborative research initiatives. Build and maintain strategic research partnerships with universities, research organisations, government agencies, industry and international space organisations. Contribute to international space missions and large-scale collaborative scientific programs. Lead and mentor research staff and Higher Degree by Research candidates, fostering the development of the next generation of planetary scientists. Develop and deliver innovative undergraduate and postgraduate teaching programs in planetary science, astrobiology and remote sensing of planetary surfaces. Lead curriculum innovation, including the development of new units and courses aligned with emerging priorities in planetary exploration and space science. Promote Curtin and the SSTC through public engagement, media activities, keynote presentations and professional service roles nationally and internationally. Contribute to School, Faculty and University leadership activities as required. Who we are looking for (Selection Criteria): Essential Qualifications PhD in Planetary Science, Space Science, Astrobiology, Planetary Geology, Remote Sensing or a closely related discipline. International Research Standing An internationally recognised research profile in planetary science demonstrated through a substantial publication record, including publications in leading journals such as the Nature and/or Science family of journals. Significant postdoctoral and academic experience gained across multiple internationally recognised research institutions or laboratories. Research Leadership and Funding Success A sustained and successful track record of securing nationally and internationally competitive research funding and leading externally funded research programs. Evidence of successful leadership of research groups, centres, institutes or major collaborative research programs. Demonstrated excellence in Higher Degree by Research supervision, including completions and mentorship of early and mid-career researchers. Planetary Science Expertise Demonstrated expertise in planetary science research, including experience in one or more of meteorite science, cometary science, planetary surface processes, astrobiology or planetary remote sensing. Significant field research experience, including undertaking and leading scientific field campaigns in remote or challenging environments. Demonstrated involvement in international space missions or major planetary science programs, including experience as a Science Team member or equivalent scientific leadership role. Teaching and Curriculum Leadership A strong record of teaching excellence in planetary science and related disciplines, including experience coordinating units, establishing new courses and developing innovative curricula. Demonstrated capability to teach planetary science, planetary remote sensing and astrobiology at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Academic Leadership and Engagement Outstanding communication, stakeholder engagement and public speaking skills, including the ability to engage effectively with scientific, industry, government and community audiences. Demonstrated commitment to collegiality, diversity, inclusion, integrity and research excellence. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia. We will also consider international candidates who are eligible for a relevant work visa. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check Your new Team – School of Earth and Planetary Sciences: The School incorporates geology, spatial sciences, advanced analytical techniques, geophysics, planetary science, and more, to provide world-class training for planetary, geoscientific and geospatial careers, and undertake fundamental and applied research into the past, present and future of our planet and solar system, recognised by the latest Excellence in Research for Australia assessment. With an impressive suite of facilities and expertise, and a range of funding from academic and industry sources, our School undertakes research projects all over the world in most fields of geoscience and planetary science. A core value of our school is to deliver teaching excellence for undergraduate and Higher Degree by Research students, which is presented in a broad field of units and courses. For more information about the school, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/learning-teaching/science-engineering/school-of-earth-and-planetary-sciences/ Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace: At Curtin University, we are passionate about fostering an inclusive, diverse and welcoming environment in which all people feel respected, valued and safe. Curtin is dedicated to promoting gender equity and inclusivity in all areas. We are proud of our achievements to date and recognise that there is more to do to improve employment outcomes for women and people of diverse gender identities, particularly in STEMM disciplines. As an Athena SWAN accredited institution, we celebrate diversity and actively work to advance the representation of women and gender-diverse people in STEMM areas and empower individuals so they can thrive in their careers through a range of supportive initiatives, including leadership training, mentorship for early career progression and promotion, networking opportunities, and other professional development resources. As reflected in the Athena Swan Charter, gender equality is a broader issue that extends beyond barriers affecting women alone, and we are committed to addressing these challenges holistically. Additionally, we offer staff a competitive remuneration and benefits package, including but not limited to: 17% superannuation, plus a variety of salary packaging options. Paid Parental leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver and 3 weeks for non-primary) subject to eligibility criteria outlined in the Enterprise Agreement; personal leave (14 days) and annual leave (20 days) Enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year (including Easter Tuesday!), and 2 extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year Period (see EBA 51.1 and 51.9). Breastfeeding facilities and campus-based childcare (where priority of placement is given to Curtin staff and students) Flexible work arrangements including return to work provisions On-campus medical centre Academic Carer Support Scheme and other diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives detailed via the below link. We believe that diverse perspectives fuel innovation and drive excellence, and we encourage women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people of diverse gender, sex and sexuality, people with disability and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds to join us in shaping the future of STEMM together and making tomorrow better. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This continuing ALD level role attracts a remuneration of $178,845 - $196,832 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a variety food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience A document (up to 1500 words) addressing the selection criteria for the role (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Wednesday, 14 October 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. #LI-DNI Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=908240": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer - Social Work",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer - Social Work Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 24/9/2026 Full time, permanent | Curtin Bentley Campus Be part of an exciting and dynamic social work team Great benefits and work environment Reference Number: 1676199 About the Role: The Curtin School of Allied Health is seeking to appoint a permanent Teaching and Research (T&R) Academic, Senior Lecturer in Social Work. This role is central to the Curtin School of Allied Health’s commitment to excellence in scholarly teaching practices and research standing across the Bachelor of Social Work and Master of Social Work (Qualifying). We are seeking a critically reflective and student-centred social work educator and researcher who can design and deliver high-quality teaching across a range of content areas and lead and contribute to research and higher degree supervision. Collegiality is important at both the social work program level and the broader School level, including through active contribution to program development and interprofessional practice across the Curtin School of Allied Health (CSAH). The successful applicant will bring relevant social work expertise and a commitment to the continued growth, quality and impact of our social work curriculum and program research. Your responsibilities will include: Teaching Design and deliver inclusive, engaging learning activities across various formats (online, face-to-face) to enhance student learning outcomes and readiness for practice. Collaborate with colleagues to innovate curriculum and develop effective learning materials, while assessing student needs and providing necessary support. Maintaining awareness of current discipline practices and contributing to professional development in higher education. Assess and provide feedback on learning to students. Manage administrative tasks related to course delivery, including assessment moderation, and ensure that curricular innovations accommodate diverse student needs for a successful university experience. Participate as an active member of the social work program and CSAH in meetings, working groups and engagement activities as required. Research Play a significant role in research projects, including, where appropriate, leadership of a research team/project. Actively seek and act as co or chief investigator in applications for competitive research grants and/or fellowships. Disseminate research findings through seminars, workshops and conferences. Publish/exhibit in high-quality refereed journals/outlets in manner consistent with disciplinary practice. Undertake principal supervision of research honours, research masters, and research doctoral students. Provide research support and mentoring of social work colleagues as appropriate. Service and Leadership Provide leadership in relation to school/faculty–wide initiatives Contribute to academic engagement and collegiality, in fostering a supportive working environment. Participate in community engagement and external activities relevant to social work. Demonstrate professional behaviours consistent with the AASW Code of Ethics and the University Code of Conduct and Vision, Mission and Values. If you are passionate about social work, we encourage you to apply and become a part of our dedicated team at Curtin University. Your expertise and commitment will help us shape the future of social work education. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Eligibility for membership of the Australian Association of Social Workers (AASW) A doctoral qualification in social work or a discipline relevant to social work. Demonstrated knowledge and experience of pedagogies appropriate to and in keeping with contemporary educational practice relevant to social work. Demonstrated experience in curriculum development and unit/course coordination. Demonstrated high level communication and interpersonal and organisational skills Demonstrated commitment to the development of a collegial and supportive working environment and the ability to interact with students and staff with sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to the application of relevant and applicable policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the functions of this position. An established record of research outputs in refereed academic journals/outlets consistent with social work. Record of obtaining research income, including competitive research grants and/or fellowships. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new team: School of Allied Health The School of Allied Health at Curtin University is a vibrant, collaborative, and community-focused school dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of people across Western Australia and beyond. Our teaching, research, and partnerships span a wide range of allied health disciplines, including exercise and sport science, occupational therapy, physiotherapy, speech pathology and social work. We are deeply committed to delivering an exceptional student experience - one that prepares graduates to be compassionate, skilled, and practice-ready professionals who make meaningful contributions to the communities they serve. Our staff are passionate educators, researchers, and practitioners who work closely with industry partners, health services, and community organisations to ensure our programs remain relevant, innovative, and responsive to emerging health needs. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a full time permanent, teaching & research academic position, with a salary range between $148,847 - $171,344 (ALC) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Thursday, 22 October 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=908469": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Social Work",
      "description": "Lecturer - Social Work Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Part Time 24/9/2026 Part time (0.6 FTE), permanent | Curtin Bentley Campus Be part of an exciting and dynamic social work team Great benefits and work environment Reference Number: 1677374 About the Role: The Curtin School of Allied Health is seeking to appoint a permanent Teaching Academic Lecturer in Social Work, at level B (0.6FTE). This role is central to the Curtin School of Allied Health’s commitment to excellence in teaching and learning, and to providing a rigorous, inclusive and engaging student experience across the Bachelor of Social Work and Master of Social Work (Qualifying). We are seeking critically reflective and student-centred social work practitioners and/or educators who can design and deliver high-quality teaching across a range of content areas, coordinate units effectively, support diverse learners, and contribute to curriculum quality, innovation and the scholarship of learning and teaching. Collegiality is important at both the social work program level and the broader School level, including through active contribution to program development and interprofessional practice across the Curtin School of Allied Health (CSAH). The successful applicant will bring relevant social work expertise, contemporary practice knowledge, and a commitment to contributing to the continued growth, quality and impact of our social work programs. Your responsibilities will include: Design and deliver inclusive, engaging learning activities across various formats (online, face-to-face) to enhance student learning outcomes and readiness for practice. Collaborate with colleagues to innovate curriculum and develop effective learning materials, while assessing student needs and providing necessary support. Maintaining awareness of current discipline practices and contributing to professional development in higher education. Assess and provide feedback on learning to students. Manage administrative tasks related to course delivery, including assessment moderation, and ensure that curricular innovations accommodate diverse student needs for a successful university experience. Participate as an active member of the social work program and CSAH in meetings, working groups and engagement activities as required. If you are passionate about social work, we encourage you to apply and become a part of our dedicated team at Curtin University. Your expertise and commitment will help us shape the future of social work education. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Eligibility for membership of the Australian Association of Social Workers (AASW) Masters degree or PhD. We welcome applicants who may not hold a Masters or PhD qualification; please outline your equivalent experience in your cover letter with reference to Level 9 in the following document: https://tinyurl.com/3r6yfaz4 (please copy and paste link into your search browser). An understanding of or willingness to develop knowledge of pedagogy and contemporary educational practice relevant to social work. Demonstrated capacity / experience to coordinate activities and/or projects with the ability to apply these to unit coordination. Demonstrated high level communication and interpersonal and organisational skills Demonstrated commitment to the development of a collegial and supportive working environment and the ability to interact with students and staff with sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to the application of relevant and applicable policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the functions of this position. Understanding of and demonstrated commitment to principles of social justice, critical perspectives in social work and social work ethics. Demonstrated capacity to facilitate learning using a reflective approach. Desirable An emerging record of contribution to scholarship in teaching. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new team: School of Allied Health The School of Allied Health at Curtin University is a vibrant, collaborative, and community-focused school dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of people across Western Australia and beyond. Our teaching, research, and partnerships span a wide range of allied health disciplines, including exercise and sport science, occupational therapy, physiotherapy, speech pathology and social work. We are deeply committed to delivering an exceptional student experience - one that prepares graduates to be compassionate, skilled, and practice-ready professionals who make meaningful contributions to the communities they serve. Our staff are passionate educators, researchers, and practitioners who work closely with industry partners, health services, and community organisations to ensure our programs remain relevant, innovative, and responsive to emerging health needs. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a part time (0.6 FTE) permanent, teaching academic position, with a salary range between $ 121,857 - $144,352 (ALB) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Thursday, 22 October 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
