    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916399": {
      "title": "Director of Global Partnerships",
      "description": "Director of Global Partnerships Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor International Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 15/10/2026 Full-time leadership appointment until 21 September 2027 Based at Curtin's Bentley Campus with engagement across our Global Campus networ Competitive Remuneration package, on campus gym, medical centre and more Job Reference: 1695014 Help shape Curtin’s global connections through partnerships that create lasting educational, research and commercial impact. Curtin University is seeking a strategic and collaborative leader to join Global Curtin as Director of Global Partnerships. Reporting directly to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Global, this is a senior leadership role with visibility across University’s executive. This is an opportunity to broaden your leadership experience and contribute to the University’s international direction. Working with colleagues across Curtin and partners around the world, you will strengthen established relationships, pursue new opportunities and support the continued growth and impact of our Global Campus network. This position is being offered on a full-time, fixed term basis until 21 September 2027. The Opportunity As Director, Global Partnerships, you will lead the development and management of strategic relationships with universities, sponsor organisations, governments, non-profit organisations and industry partners. You will bring together academic priorities, commercial opportunities and local market knowledge to build sustainable partnerships that advance Curtin’s strategic objectives and international reputation. A key focus will be building and strengthening the articulation arrangements and international partnerships that underpin Curtin’s Transnational Education (TNE) activity. Supported by a skilled team, you will develop sustainable partnerships that deliver lasting value for Curtin. You will work closely with academic and professional leaders to turn opportunities into practical, well-governed initiatives, balancing ambition with quality, financial sustainability and effective risk management. In this role, you will: Shape our global partnership strategy , identifying priority relationships and opportunities to strengthen Curtin’s international reach and impact. Lead partnership development and negotiations , securing agreements that deliver mutual value and protect the University’s interests. Advance TNE and Global Campus initiatives , providing strategic advice on new programs, commercial opportunities and potential campus activity. Connect people and opportunities across Curtin , enabling joint academic programs, dual degrees, research collaboration and student and staff exchanges. Guide sound decision-making , overseeing business planning, due diligence, contracts, resources and partnership performance. Provide advice to senior leaders , translating complex market, policy and legislative requirements into clear recommendations and University policy. Lead, develop and support the Global Partnerships teams, including TNE Partnerships and Sponsorship, setting direction, managing workload and resources, and fostering a collaborative, high-performing team culture. About you You are a thoughtful relationship builder who can bring people together around shared goals and navigate complex international environments with confidence and sound judgement. You combine strategic thinking with commercial awareness and a practical commitment to delivering outcomes. You will bring: A postgraduate qualification with relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of extensive experience, education and training. Experience in international or complex university programs, contract management and policy development, or transferable experience from a comparable area of the University. Experience developing business plans, proposals and agreements, alongside due diligence, risk assessment and partnership evaluation. The ability to develop policy that supports strategic priorities and responds to legislative requirements through consultation and negotiation. Strong leadership, business negotiation and stakeholder engagement skills, including the ability to work effectively with international partners and government representatives. Excellent written communication skills, with the ability to prepare clear, persuasive reports, proposals and submissions. Experience in TNE quality management and the delivery or administration of offshore programs will be highly regarded. Role Requirements: Interstate and overseas travel may be required, including to Curtin’s Global Campuses. You will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Global Curtin Global Curtin provides University-wide leadership, planning and coordination of Curtin’s international activities. The teams work connects global partnerships, international recruitment and admissions, student sponsorship, pathways, policy and compliance to support the University’s global ambitions. For more information, please visit: https://central-int.curtin.edu.au/engage/global-engagement/one-curtin/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! Please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest in the role and how your background aligns with the selection criteria noted above If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications via this email address and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday 1 November 2026 Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916270": {
      "title": "Senior Developer",
      "description": "Senior Developer Office of Corporate Services Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 15/10/2026 Ongoing role, Full-time, Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Work-life balance options, inc. potential flexible working from home arrangement Reference: 1696080 About the Role: Join us in building the Digital Platform shaping how staff and students experience Curtin. Our Digital Experience Platform team is behind AIDA; Curtin's growing ecosystem of AI assistants built on AWS Bedrock. We're looking for a Senior Developer with a platform mindset — someone who thinks in reusable components and long-term architecture rather than one-off fixes, who's genuinely curious about applied GenAI, and who's comfortable experimenting, iterating and adapting as the technology and the University's needs evolve. As Senior Developer, you'll take a technical lead role across development and support of University applications and interfaces, utilising the latest technologies and cloud native services to drive innovative use cases forward. You will also mentor the wider development team and act as a trusted technical voice for stakeholders across the University. Your responsibilities will include: Provide technical leadership across the design, development, testing, implementation and support of AI, enterprise search and digital platform solutions. Build scalable multi-agent workflows and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines using AWS Bedrock and related cloud-native services. Evaluate foundation models, optimise prompts and apply model selection techniques to balance performance, quality and cost. Embed AI governance, security and responsible use practices through content filtering, PII masking and anti-injection safeguards. Drive DevOps and MLOps practices, including infrastructure provisioning, monitoring system health, model latency and token spend. Translate business requirements into practical solution designs, testing approaches and technical documentation. Mentor team members and communicate effectively with stakeholders to align expectations, timeframes and delivery outcomes. Selection Criteria: A relevant degree with extensive relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of experience and education/training. Extensive experience in relevant programming languages, enterprise platforms, tools and technologies, with the ability to mentor and develop team members. Knowledge of current and emerging agentic frameworks, Infrastructure as Code methodologies and cloud-native development practices. Strong organisational skills and understanding of project management methodologies, Agile delivery, tools and techniques. Well-developed written and verbal communication skills, with a track record of client focus and professional stakeholder engagement. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with a proven ability to contribute to practical business outcomes. A platform mindset, with the ability to design reusable components, scalable architecture and maintainable long-term solutions. Comfortable working across ambiguity, experimentation and shifting priorities in a fast-evolving technology landscape. Given the platforms you'll be working on, we'd love to see: Hands-on experience with Amazon Bedrock and LLM operations — working independently across areas such as RAG pipeline design, vector stores/knowledge bases, Bedrock Agents, and monitoring or optimising model performance and cost. Proven experience building autonomous agents Track record of optimising cost and building observability tools to manage enterprise costs Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous G08 level role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday, 1st November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916380": {
      "title": "Safer Community Officer - Kalgoorlie",
      "description": "Safer Community Officer - Kalgoorlie Office of Corporate Services Kalgoorlie, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 15/10/2026 Multiple full-time, continuing (permanent) opportunities | Kalgoorlie Campus Join Curtin's newly established in-house Safer Community team Attractive regional benefits including locality allowance Job Reference: 1692770 About the Role We are seeking Safer Community Officers to join Curtin University as we establish our in-house Safer Community service at the Kalgoorlie Campus. As a Safer Community Officer, you will provide a visible, approachable and reassuring presence across campus, supporting the safety, security and wellbeing of students, staff and visitors. While security is an important part of the role, Curtin’s Safer Community approach goes further. You will combine proactive patrols and incident response with strong customer service, early intervention and wellbeing support. No two shifts will be quite the same. You may be responding to a safety or security incident, supporting someone experiencing distress, providing a safety escort, assisting with events, liaising with emergency services or simply being a trusted and visible point of contact for members of the campus community. Key Responsibilities: Undertake high-visibility foot, bicycle and vehicle patrols across assigned campus areas. Provide safety escorts and assistance to members of the Curtin community. Communicate information about Safer Community services and maintain a visible and approachable presence on campus. Provide a high standard of customer service and contribute to a safe and engaging environment. Record details of incidents and unusual occurrences accurately. Assist senior colleagues in responding to emergency and non-emergency situations. Guide emergency service vehicles responding to incidents on campus. Assist with special events, functions, VIP visits and traffic management where required. Provide immediate response, triage and appropriate referral for people experiencing welfare or wellbeing concerns. Support general campus safety and security activities as required. SELECTION CRITERIA Who We Are Looking For: We are looking for people who are approachable, observant and confident communicating with a diverse community. You will bring: Completion of a Certificate II in Security Operations through a recognised security training provider. Highly developed interpersonal and communication skills. Strong customer service skills and the ability to manage difficult or challenging interactions professionally. The ability to work sensitively and effectively with people from diverse backgrounds. A flexible, self-motivated approach and the ability to work both independently and as part of a team. The ability to provide effective first-response intervention to individuals presenting with wellbeing or trauma concerns. The ability to quickly assess a situation and implement the appropriate response within established policies and procedures. Computer literacy and confidence using a range of applications and security systems. Mental Health First Aid training is desirable. Role Requirements Current WA Driver’s Licence. Current Senior First Aid Certificate. A level of fitness commensurate with the position. Ability to work outside normal office hours and participate in an operational shift roster. Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ Curtin’s Properties Facilities & Development team supports the safe, effective and reliable operation of the University’s physical environment. In Kalgoorlie, the team provides facilities and maintenance support across the campus, student accommodation and Curtin’s expanding residential housing portfolio. Working across everything from laboratories and lecture theatres to accommodation and residential properties, you will be exposed to a variety of facilities and specialised environments, while working closely with university staff, contractors and service providers to keep our facilities safe, functional and well maintained. Curtin Kalgoorlie is located in the heart of Western Australia’s historic Goldfields. Home to the renowned WA School of Mines, the campus has a strong community atmosphere and provides the opportunity to live and work in one of Australia’s most important resources regions. What We Offer You These full-time, permanent positions attract a salary package of $69,000 - $75,046 (G03) plus 17% superannuation, in addition to applicable locality and shift allowances. Build Something New: Join the team as Curtin establishes its in-house Safer Community service in Kalgoorlie and help create the culture and way of working from the outset. Additional Earning Opportunities: Participate in an operational shift roster with applicable shift allowances (TBD), with potential overtime opportunities depending on operational requirements. Rewards Beyond Salary: Enjoy 17% superannuation, locality allowance, generous rent subsidy, customisable salary packaging benefits through Paywise, as well as hotel and health insurance discounts. Time Well-Spent: Experience the added value of 17.5% leave loading, with the option to be paid out or taken as additional leave. Benefit from Parental Leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver), Annual Leave (25 days), Personal Leave (14 days), Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Leave (5 days) * Travel Leave (2 days), Study Leave and Community Service Leave. Plus 18% more observed days off – enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year, including Easter Tuesday, and two extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year period. Recognition for Prior Service: Curtin recognises prior service for long service leave purposes. New Curtin employees may be* eligible if they have worked for an Australian university, public service or other recognised institution. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join us! Please click APPLY NOW , ensuring you attach the following documents: Cover letter outlining your skills, knowledge and experience relevant to the position. Resume. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our Careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 9.00pm AWST, 29 October 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916379": {
      "title": "Safer Community Shift Supervisor - Kalgoorlie",
      "description": "Safer Community Shift Supervisor - Kalgoorlie Office of Corporate Services Kalgoorlie, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 15/10/2026 Full-time, continuing (permanent) opportunity | Kalgoorlie Campus Lead Curtin's newly established in-house Safer Community service Attractive regional benefits including locality allowance Job Reference: 1692764 About the Role We are seeking an experienced and motivated Safer Community Shift Supervisor to lead frontline operations and support the safety, security and wellbeing of the Curtin Kalgoorlie community. As Shift Supervisor, you will provide leadership and direction to a team of Safer Community Officers while overseeing day-to-day operations during rostered shifts. You will coordinate responses to safety, security and wellbeing incidents, support emergency management activities and ensure a high standard of service delivery across campus. This position combines traditional security leadership with a strong people-focused and wellbeing-centred approach. You will be joining the service as Curtin transitions Kalgoorlie security operations from an outsourced model to direct University delivery, providing an opportunity to help establish the team and contribute to how the service operates from the outset. The approved business case identifies improved student experience, greater operational oversight, enhanced governance and accountability, consistent Safer Communities service delivery and reduced operational and reputational risk as key reasons for the change. The role will oversee an operational shift roster, while working Monday to Friday as a non-shift worker. Key Responsibilities: Lead, supervise and support Safer Community Officers during rostered shifts. Conduct team briefings and coordinate the appropriate deployment of team members across campus. Oversee the Kalgoorlie Communications Centre, including CCTV, access control, fire alarm and communication systems. Coordinate responses to safety, security, emergency and wellbeing incidents. Provide leadership and direction during emergency and critical incidents. Ensure appropriate escalation and notification processes are followed. Liaise with emergency services and relevant University stakeholders. Respond to wellbeing needs with empathy and provide appropriate support, information and referral pathways. Prepare and maintain accurate incident reports and operational records. Contribute to continuous improvement initiatives and the ongoing development of Safer Community operations. SELECTION CRITERIA Who We Are Looking For: You will be an experienced security professional with the confidence to lead others, make sound decisions under pressure and provide a calm and professional response across a diverse range of operational situations. You will bring: Completion of a Certificate IV or higher in Security Operations through a recognised security training provider, together with extensive relevant experience. Demonstrated ability to lead and supervise a team responsible for customer service and maintaining a safe and secure environment. Strong judgement and the ability to prioritise, initiate action and respond rapidly and appropriately. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, including the ability to work professionally with people experiencing distress, conflict or challenging circumstances. The ability to supervise and work effectively as part of a unified team. Strong written communication and incident reporting skills. Confidence using electronic communication tools, security systems and Microsoft applications. Initiative, resilience and the ability to maintain professionalism under pressure. Role Requirements Current WA Driver’s Licence. Current First Aid Certificate. Mental Health First Aid Training. A level of fitness commensurate with the position. Ability to work outside normal office hours and participate in an operational shift roster. Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ Curtin’s Properties Facilities & Development team supports the safe, effective and reliable operation of the University’s physical environment. In Kalgoorlie, the team provides facilities and maintenance support across the campus, student accommodation and Curtin’s expanding residential housing portfolio. Working across everything from laboratories and lecture theatres to accommodation and residential properties, you will be exposed to a variety of facilities and specialised environments, while working closely with university staff, contractors and service providers to keep our facilities safe, functional and well maintained. Curtin Kalgoorlie is located in the heart of Western Australia’s historic Goldfields. Home to the renowned WA School of Mines, the campus has a strong community atmosphere and provides the opportunity to live and work in one of Australia’s most important resources regions. What We Offer You These full-time, permanent positions attract a salary package of $85,781 - $93,836 (G05) plus 17% superannuation, in addition to applicable locality and shift allowances. Make Your Mark: Join Curtin as we establish our in-house Safer Community service in Kalgoorlie and help shape a strong, connected and community-focused operation. Additional Earning Opportunities: Participate in an operational shift roster with applicable shift allowances, with potential overtime opportunities depending on operational requirements. Rewards Beyond Salary: Enjoy 17% superannuation, locality allowance, generous rent subsidy, customisable salary packaging benefits through Paywise, as well as hotel and health insurance discounts. Time Well-Spent: Experience the added value of 17.5% leave loading, with the option to be paid out or taken as additional leave. Benefit from Parental Leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver), Annual Leave (20 days), Personal Leave (14 days), Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Leave (5 days)*, Travel Leave (2 days), Study Leave and Community Service Leave. Plus 18% more observed days off – enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year, including Easter Tuesday, and two extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year period. Recognition for Prior Service: Curtin recognises prior service for long service leave purposes. New Curtin employees may be* eligible if they have worked for an Australian university, public service or other recognised institution. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join us! Please click APPLY NOW , ensuring you attach the following documents: Cover letter outlining your skills, knowledge and experience relevant to the position. Resume. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our Careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 9.00pm AWST, 29 October 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916267": {
      "title": "Awards Advisor, Graduations",
      "description": "Awards Advisor, Graduations Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 15/10/2026 Full time, fixed term opportunity to September 2027 | Bentley Campus On campus Gym & childcare, wellbeing program, excellent leave entitlements Support students across awards, conferral, assessment and graduation Job Reference: 1693800 About the Role Working within the Service Delivery directorate, you will play an important role in supporting Curtin's awards and graduation activities and assisting with the coordination of graduation ceremonies. You will also provide high-quality Tier 2 support to students across a range of matters relating to assessment, examinations, academic status, conferral and awards, with a special focus on all graduation matters. You will work closely with colleagues across Student Services, faculties and schools to resolve complex enquiries and ensure students receive accurate, timely and professional advice. This is a varied role suited to someone who enjoys working in a busy service environment, balancing detailed administrative work with stakeholder engagement and delivering an excellent student experience. Key Responsibilities: Assist with the coordination and delivery of graduation events. Manage complex student enquiries across face-to-face, online and telephone channels. Provide advice and support relating to assessments, examinations, academic status, conferral and awards. Build effective relationships with faculty and other key stakeholders. Communicate with students regarding conferral and the provision of awards. Identify opportunities to improve processes and procedures to enhance the student experience. SELECTION CRITERIA Who we are looking for: A degree; or a diploma with considerable relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience providing high-quality customer service and a commitment to delivering an excellent client experience. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. Project management experience will be looked upon favourably. Well-developed problem-solving skills and the ability to use sound judgement and discretion. Strong interpersonal skills, the ability to build effective working relationships with stakeholders across different areas and organisational levels, and confidence to present to large groups of stakeholders. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a strong view to ensure positive end-user experience. The ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Strong digital literacy across a range of common business applications. Previous experience working in a student-focused environment, using a student information system such as Student One, working with a CRM, or supporting events would be highly regarded. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed term role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Student Services Student Services aim to enable Curtin’s vision through the delivery of a seamless student-centred experience accessible at any time, on any device, from any location. The area is responsible for the delivery of a suite of essential services, processes and systems which underpin and drive core Curtin University operations. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full-time, continuing role attracts a salary package of $85,781 - $93,836 (G05) per annum plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience, addressing the Selection Criteria noted above If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00PM (AWST), 29 October 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916391": {
      "title": "Communications & Engagement Specialist",
      "description": "Communications & Engagement Specialist Office of Corporate Services Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 15/10/2026 Fixed-term, 12-month opportunity | Curtin Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference: 1677386 About the Role: The Communications and Engagement Specialist sits within Corporate Communications and provides communications and engagement support across the Faculty of Science & Engineering. Corporate Communications provides strategic direction, prioritisation and oversight, while the Faculty provides local context, priorities and access to stakeholders, with activity delivered through a coordinated, shared approach. This role is established to lead the planning and delivery of communications and stakeholder engagement for Curtin's WA School of Mines 125th anniversary milestone year and the launch of new Faculty infrastructure projects, as well as responding to other significant Faculty communications and engagement priorities as they arise during the term of the appointment. The role operates across the full breadth of the communications mix, from day-to-day content and channel delivery through to big-picture narrative, positioning, stakeholder engagement and reputation management. You will perform work guided by policy, precedent and professional standards, exercise sound judgement on sensitive or reputationally significant matters and have discretion to innovate to achieve objectives. Your responsibilities will include: Plan and deliver communications strategies: Plan, develop and implement communications strategies for key Faculty priorities and activities. Lead integrated strategy: Lead the development of integrated media, communications and stakeholder engagement strategy for designated Faculty priorities and projects, connecting the Faculty's narrative to Curtin's broader institutional positioning and reputation. Deliver on milestone priorities: Lead the planning and delivery of communications and engagement for the WA School of Mines 125th anniversary milestone year and the launch of new Faculty infrastructure projects and respond to other significant Faculty communications and engagement priorities as they arise. Produce content across channels: Research, write and edit communications/promotional material for a variety of audiences across a range of channels, including print and digital (website, social media platforms and e-newsletters). Maintain quality and brand consistency: Ensure all written content is of high quality; aligned with Curtin's vision, mission and values; accurate and consistent across all publishing channels; and complies with Curtin brand identity style guidelines. Evaluate and improve: Evaluate and report on the effectiveness of communication strategies and seek to continuously improve communication and engagement with relevant audiences. Stakeholder engagement: Build and manage a stakeholder engagement plan covering industry, alumni, government, community and internal audiences relevant to the Faculty's priority initiatives, identifying opportunities and risks and coordinating engagement activity. Act as primary interface: Act as the primary interface between the Faculty and Corporate Communications for these priority initiatives, leveraging existing media and strategic communications expertise to undertake other activities consistent with the accountabilities above. Who we are looking for/Selection Criteria: A relevant degree in communications, public relations, marketing or a related discipline and relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. Proven ability to plan, implement and evaluate communications and engagement strategies. Proven project management and stakeholder engagement skills. Demonstrated experience in managing digital communications channels and web content. Demonstrated interpersonal skills with the ability to communicate sensitively and constructively and to develop relationships across all levels of an organisation. Demonstrated professional writing and communication skills, with experience in the preparation of clear, accurate and timely communication materials, targeted at a diverse audience. Demonstrated organisational skills and ability to set priorities and meet deadlines. Demonstrated ability to work independently as well as effectively as part of a team. Experience in the science, engineering or mining sectors is desirable. Experience working in the tertiary education sector or a large, complex organisation is desirable. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term G08 level role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 5pm (AWST), Sunday 1st of November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
